  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nPeeks of sunshine have begun to filter through the gloomy\nstratus that was in place for much of the morning. This has \nallowed temperatures to rise into the 70s in areas that have had\nseveral hours of scattered clouds. Our rainy weather will stay \non hold while surface high pressure spreads across the Upper MS \nValley tonight into Monday. The light winds associated with the\nhigh pressure in combination with 60F Tds should support \nanother night of patchy fog development similar to this \nmorning.\n\nWe'll continue to see air and dewpoint temps rise as return flow \nincreases on the backside of the high pressure Monday. This will\nset the stage for yet another round of showers that spread from\nwest to east ahead of a weak mid-level wave. The main change to\nthe forecast is an increase in QPF from Tuesday all of the way \nthrough Thursday now, with the latest NBM producing a solid 1-2\"\nfor much of southeast Minnesota into Wisconsin. The anomalous \nmoisture of 1.5\" PWATs (over 200% of normal for this time of \nyear) is a key contributor to the rainfall potential despite \nrelatively weaker forcing. All that said, confidence is lower \nthan before for rainfall amounts with the bulk of that increase \nin QPF being driven by an outlier in the model suite. If you \nhave been following weather recently, you probably can guess \nwhich model that is before I even write it down. If you guessed \nthe GFS, you would be correct. It's ensemble and deterministic \nsolutions generally place the stalled front on Wednesday a few \nhundred miles north of its European and Canadian counterparts. \nWe tend to see the ECMWF AIFS perform well with the placement of\nheavy precip for our area at this timeframe (24+ hours out), \nwhich would favor the greatest footprint being across Iowa and \nSE Minnesota into Wisconsin. Even in this scenario, we do expect\nto see the rain showers move from west to east on Tuesday, the \nlow confidence part of the forecast is just with how far south \nthat front stalls out and if we stay largely dry or see another \nfull day of rain Wednesday.\n\nNorthwest flow and ridging will eventually build in to end the\nweek, resulting in a cooler and drier Canadian air mass \nspreading south. Any rain chances will be tied to more subtle \nwaves within the flow, meaning there is no clear consensus on \nwhen, where, or even how much we will see next weekend. The \nhigher confidence part of the forecast is that it'll become a \nbreezier period as winds shift out of the northwest and advect \nin the cooler airmass.",
